        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1537 CE – 1598 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Born</span><span class="info-stat-val">According to tradition, 17 March 1537 (the 6th day of the 2nd month of Tenbun 6) in Nakamura, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owari-province">Owari Province</a>, now Nakamura Ward in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagoya">Nagoya</a>; son of Kinoshita Yaemon, an ashigaru, with no traceable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> lineage</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Died</span><span class="info-stat-val">18 September 1598 at Fushimi Castle; his death was kept secret by the Council of Five Elders to preserve morale</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Who they were</span><span class="info-stat-val">Retainer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> from 1573; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sessho-and-kampaku">kampaku</a> (Imperial Regent) from 1585, the first commoner to hold the post; daijo-daijin (Chancellor of the Realm) from 1586; taiko (retired regent) from 1592 until his death</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">avenging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-yamazaki">Battle of Yamazaki</a> in 1582; completing the unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> by 1591; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka-castle">Osaka Castle</a>; the sword hunt of 1588; the land surveys and the class system; the Bateren Edict against Christian missionaries; the invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> from 1592; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japanese-tea-ceremony">tea ceremony</a> and the Golden <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">Tea</a> Room</span></div>
