    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Born with no surname to a peasant who served as a foot soldier, Hideyoshi rose through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>'s service and, after Nobunaga's death in 1582, became the first commoner to hold the post of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sessho-and-kampaku">kampaku</a>.</li><li>He subdued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a> and the Kanto and finished the unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1591, disarmed the peasantry, and ordered land surveys and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> that formed the basis of systematic taxation.</li><li>His invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> from 1592 ended in stalemate and exhaustion, and after his death in 1598 his young son Hideyori was displaced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>, who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Hideyoshi never took the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a>. He arranged to be adopted by Konoe Sakihisa, one of the highest-ranking members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-clan">Fujiwara clan</a>, and in 1586 the Imperial Court gave him the new clan name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

