    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1537</span><span class="rf-text">Hideyoshi is born in Nakamura, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owari-province">Owari Province</a>, according to tradition, the son of an ashigaru</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1558</span><span class="rf-text">He becomes an ashigaru of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-clan">Oda clan</a>, then headed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>, and soon Nobunaga's sandal-bearer</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1573</span><span class="rf-text">Nobunaga appoints him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> of three districts in northern Omi Province, where he builds Nagahama Castle</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1582</span><span class="rf-text">Nobunaga dies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honno-ji">Honno-ji</a> incident; Hideyoshi defeats the assassin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akechi-mitsuhide">Akechi Mitsuhide</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-yamazaki">Battle of Yamazaki</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1585</span><span class="rf-text">He becomes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sessho-and-kampaku">kampaku</a> and conquers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka-castle">Osaka Castle</a>'s main enclosure is finished</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1590</span><span class="rf-text">The Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawara">Odawara</a> against the Later Hojo; the following year the Kunohe rebellion completes the unification</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1592</span><span class="rf-text">He takes the title of taiko and launches the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1598</span><span class="rf-text">Hideyoshi dies at Fushimi Castle; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Battle of Sekigahara</a> in 1600 displaces his son Hideyori</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga.jpg" alt="Oda Nobunaga" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Odanobunaga.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">狩野宗秀 (Kanō Sōshū, 1551 - 1601), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>According to tradition, Hideyoshi was born on 17 March 1537 (the 6th day of the 2nd month of Tenbun 6) in Nakamura, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owari-province">Owari Province</a>, now Nakamura Ward in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagoya">Nagoya</a>, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku period</a>, as the Ashikaga <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> collapsed. He had no traceable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> lineage. His father, Kinoshita Yaemon, was an ashigaru, a peasant employed by samurai as a foot soldier, and Hideyoshi had no surname. His childhood given name was Hiyoshimaru, though variations exist. Yaemon died in 1543, when Hideyoshi was seven years old.</p><p>Contemporary writings by Ankokuji Ekei and by Kakukane Ue, a retainer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimazu-clan">Shimazu clan</a>, confirm his poor background, and Ekei noted that he had begged in the streets. Many legends describe his being sent to study at a temple as a young man; he rejected temple life and went in search of adventure. Under the name Kinoshita Tokichiro he first joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imagawa-clan">Imagawa clan</a> as a servant to a local ruler named Matsushita Yukitsuna. In 1558 he became an ashigaru for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-clan">Oda clan</a>, the rulers of his home province of Owari, then headed by the ambitious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>. He soon became Nobunaga's sandal-bearer, a position of relatively high status. His biographers say he also managed the kitchen and supervised repairs to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiyosu">Kiyosu</a> Castle, though the latter claim has been called "apocryphal".</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Toyotomi Hideyoshi&#8221; cites where it covers Early life (1537–1558):</p><ul><li>Stephen Turnbull, Toyotomi Hideyoshi (Osprey Publishing, 2010)</li></ul></details><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Family in the encyclopedia</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/encyclopedia/yodo-dono">Yodo-dono<span>Married</span></a></p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gifu-castle"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gifu-castle.jpg" alt="Gifu Castle" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gifu-castle">Gifu Castle</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gifu_Castle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Alpsdake, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1561 Hideyoshi married Nene, the adopted daughter of Asano Nagakatsu. In 1564 he found success as a negotiator, persuading several Mino warlords to desert the Saito clan, largely through bribery, among them the clan's strategist Takenaka Shigeharu. Nobunaga's easy victory at the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gifu-castle">Inabayama Castle</a> in 1567 was largely due to his efforts, and in 1568 he became one of Nobunaga's most distinguished generals and took the name Hashiba Hideyoshi. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-anegawa">Battle of Anegawa</a> in 1570 he was assigned to lead Oda troops into open battle for the first time. In 1573 Nobunaga appointed him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> of three districts in northern Omi Province; he built Nagahama Castle and took control of the gunsmithing village of Kunitomo, whose output of firearms increased dramatically under his administration. Later Nobunaga sent him to conquer the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chugoku-region">Chugoku</a> region from the Mori clan.</p><p>On 21 June 1582, during the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takamatsu">Takamatsu</a>, Nobunaga and his eldest son Nobutada were killed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honno-ji">Honno-ji</a> incident. Hideyoshi made peace with the Mori and, eleven days after Nobunaga's death, defeated the assassin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akechi-mitsuhide">Akechi Mitsuhide</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-yamazaki">Battle of Yamazaki</a> on 2 July 1582, taking Nobunaga's authority and power for himself. He chose Nobunaga's infant grandson Oda Hidenobu as heir, and in 1583 destroyed the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shibata-katsuie">Shibata Katsuie</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-shizugatake">Battle of Shizugatake</a>, after which he controlled some 30 provinces. The same year he began <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka-castle">Osaka Castle</a> on the site of the temple Ishiyama Hongan-ji. In 1584 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-komaki-and-nagakute">Battle of Komaki and Nagakute</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobukatsu">Oda Nobukatsu</a> ended in a stalemate, although Hideyoshi's forces suffered heavy losses.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu.jpg" alt="Tokugawa Ieyasu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tokugawa_Ieyasu2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kanō Tan'yū, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Like Nobunaga before him, Hideyoshi never achieved the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a>. He arranged to be adopted by Konoe Sakihisa and secured the post of Imperial Regent (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sessho-and-kampaku">kampaku</a>) in 1585 and Chancellor (daijo-daijin) in 1586, when the Imperial Court gave him the clan name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a>. He built the Jurakudai palace in 1587 and entertained the reigning Emperor Go-Yozei the following year. After the earthquake of 1586 forced him to abandon a campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a>, he sent his sister and his mother to Ieyasu as hostages, and Ieyasu travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a> and expressed his intention to submit. His forces took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chosokabe-motochika">Chosokabe Motochika</a> in 1585 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimazu-clan">Shimazu clan</a> in 1587, with 200,000 soldiers against the Shimazu's 30,000. He won the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawara">Odawara</a> in 1590 and, in 1591, crushed the Kunohe rebellion, which completed the unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><p>In 1588 he forbade peasants to own weapons and launched a sword hunt; the confiscated weapons were melted down into building material for the Hall of the Great Buddha at the Hoko temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>. The measure effectively stopped peasant revolts and ensured greater stability at the expense of the freedom of the individual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a>. He ordered comprehensive land surveys and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, required <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> to live in castle towns, and in 1590 banned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, though contract and indentured labour persisted. He lavished time and money on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japanese-tea-ceremony">tea ceremony</a> and had a portable Golden <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">Tea</a> Room made.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sen-no-rikyu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sen-no-rikyu.jpg" alt="Sen no Rikyū" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sen-no-rikyu">Sen no Rikyū</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sen_no_Rikyu_JPN_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">painted by 長谷川等伯, calligraphy by 春屋宗園, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hideyoshi's rise and rule met resistance, and some of his measures were violent. In 1585 his forces attacked Negoro-ji, set the complex aflame and cut down monks as they escaped the burning buildings. An uprising in the Yokote Basin in 1590 opposed the land survey conducted by his government; it was suppressed and proved disastrous for the Onodera clan. At the Kunohe rebellion in 1591 the rebel leader Kunohe Masazane surrendered and was executed with the castle defenders. After Komaki and Nagakute, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a>'s general Sakakibara Yasumasa circulated a manifesto condemning Hideyoshi's conduct as a betrayal of Nobunaga's wishes and insulting his origins, and Hideyoshi offered 100,000 kan for the general's head. In 1591 he ordered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> master <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sen-no-rikyu">Sen no Rikyu</a> to commit suicide, possibly in a fit of anger.</p><p>In 1587 he issued the Bateren Edict ordering the expulsion of Christian missionaries. The edict is described as a response to several perceived threats, among them Catholic forces that, according to the historian Paul E. Eckel, had sacked Buddhist temples in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a>, and the trafficking of people; according to the historian Arata Harumasa, at least 50,000 Japanese were sold overseas as slaves around that time, mainly by Portuguese merchants. In late 1596 he had twenty-six Christians arrested; they were tortured, mutilated and paraded through towns, and on 5 February they were crucified in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a>.</p><p>His nephew Hidetsugu, whom he had adopted and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sessho-and-kampaku">kampaku</a>, was in August 1595 stripped of his titles, exiled to Mount Koya and ordered to commit suicide, which he did on 20 August. Hideyoshi then had members of Hidetsugu's family, including 31 women and several children, beheaded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hideyoshi's only son, three-year-old Tsurumatsu, died in September 1591, after his half-brother Hidenaga had died of illness in February. He adopted his nephew Hidetsugu in January 1592 and resigned as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sessho-and-kampaku">kampaku</a> to take the title of taiko. Adopting Nobunaga's dream of a Japanese conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, he began an invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> in 1592, intending to conquer Korea and eventually Ming China. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a> fell to Konishi Yukinaga on 19 June, and within four months his forces had reached the border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>. A Ming army sent in 1593 recaptured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a>, and the Japanese navy was destroyed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yi-sun-sin">Admiral Yi</a> Sun-sin, which ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s ability to resupply its troops in Seoul. In 1593 Hideyoshi's second son Hideyori was born.</p><p>Konishi Yukinaga and the Ming envoy Shen Weijing forged Hideyoshi's memorial to the Ming court, on which basis the Ming agreed to invest him as King of Japan. When the deception came to light in 1596, he was enraged, and in 1597 he ordered a renewed invasion, which met with less success; Japanese troops remained pinned down in Gyeongsang Province. All three parties to the war were exhausted. Hideyoshi told his commander in Korea, "Don't let my soldiers become spirits in a foreign land."</p><p>He died at Fushimi Castle on 18 September 1598. His last words to his closest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> and generals were "I depend upon you for everything. I have no other thoughts to leave behind. It is sad to part from you." The Council of Five Elders kept his death secret and ordered the forces in Korea to return to Japan.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/fukushima-masanori"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fukushima-masanori.jpg" alt="Fukushima Masanori" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/fukushima-masanori">Fukushima Masanori</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Masanori_Fukushima.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After Hideyoshi's death the Council of Five Elders was unable to keep <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>'s ambitions in check. Kato Kiyomasa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukushima-masanori">Fukushima Masanori</a>, who had served in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, returned to find <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari">Ishida Mitsunari</a> in power, held in contempt by them, and sided with Ieyasu. Hideyori lost the power his father had held, and Ieyasu's Eastern Army defeated Mitsunari's Western Army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Battle of Sekigahara</a> in 1600. Appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a> in 1603, Ieyasu attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka-castle">Osaka Castle</a> in 1614 and 1615, forcing Hideyori and his mother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yodo-dono">Yodo-dono</a> to commit suicide, which ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi clan</a>. Historians generally attribute the weakening of the regime mainly to the loss of all his adult heirs, which left only the five-year-old Hideyori.</p><p>Hideyoshi changed Japanese society through a rigid class structure, restrictions on travel and surveys of land and production; the land surveys formed the basis for systematic taxation. Ieyasu left in place the majority of his decrees and built his shogunate upon them. Hideyoshi also influenced material culture. During the Korean campaigns large quantities of prized ceramic ware were confiscated and many Korean artisans were forcibly relocated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. Politically he set up a system that balanced the most powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a>, with a council of the most influential lords and a regent in command.</p><p>Osaka Castle, the sword hunt and the temples he built and restored in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> remained. By 1915 he had been posthumously raised to Senior First Rank. In a letter to his wife he wrote that he was ready for a long siege and wished to return in triumph and leave a great name behind him.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Toyotomi Hideyoshi&#8221; cites where it covers Legacy:</p><ul><li>Jurgis Elisonas, Toyotomi Hideyoshi (Oxford University Press, 2003)</li></ul></details>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Toyotomi Hideyoshi (1537–1598), a peasant's son, rose through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>'s service, avenged him in 1582 and completed the unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> by 1591. He disarmed the peasantry and surveyed the land, then invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> in 1592; after his death <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> displaced his heir.</em></p>

